+# Lesson: No float money in `src/billing`
+
+**Rule:** In `src/billing`, money is stored and computed as **integer cents**. Don't use floats for money, and don't use `round()` on floats to "fix" a total.
+
+## What happened
+
+In the March invoice run, a total that should have been **10.30** came out as **10.29**. The float total was 10.299999…, and rounding it with `round()` didn't fix the error before it reached the invoice output.
+
+## Why it happens
+
+Binary floats can't represent most decimal amounts exactly (0.1, 0.2, 10.30, …). Summing prices as floats builds up small errors. `round(x, 2)` still returns a float, so the error stays, and any later truncation or formatting can turn 10.2999… into 10.29.
+
+## Do this
+
+- Store and pass amounts as `int` cents (e.g. `1030`, not `10.30`).
+- Sum cents with plain integer addition. No rounding needed.
+- Convert to a decimal display string only at the edge (rendering or export), e.g. `f"{cents // 100}.{cents % 100:02d}"`.
+- If a value arrives as a decimal string from outside, parse it with `decimal.Decimal` and convert to cents right away. Never go through `float`.
+
+## Don't do this
+
+```python
+# src/billing/total.py, as it stands today
+def total(items):
+    return round(sum(i["price"] for i in items), 2)   # float prices + round()
+```
+
+## Instead
+
+```python
+def total_cents(items):
+    return sum(i["price_cents"] for i in items)       # ints in, int out
+```
+
+## Review checklist for billing changes
+
+- [ ] No `float` or `round()` applied to money values
+- [ ] Field names make the unit clear (`*_cents`)
+- [ ] Tests include amounts that floats get wrong (e.g. 0.10 + 0.20, a 10.30 total)
